{
  "case_id": "2023-22-1165",
  "document_hash": "ce4305b5a3de9a04ccab4447cb40611655490206c595db76b577f5e652bba1dd",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners (Linda T. Coberly)",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1165",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce4305b5a3de9a04ccab4447cb40611655490206c595db76b577f5e652bba1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce4305b5a3de-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. COBERLY: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: Like many cases, this case should be resolved by the text, and, here, the text is in Rule 10b-5 as adopted by the Congress in the PSLRA. That text makes clear that an omission is actionable in just one circumstance, when the omitted fact is material and necessary to make a statement not misleading. Today, you're going to hear arguments for omission liability in a different circumstance, when the omitted fact is material and required to be stated by Item 303."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1165",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce4305b5a3de9a04ccab4447cb40611655490206c595db76b577f5e652bba1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce4305b5a3de-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: Can a -- a compliance certification statement be made misleading by an omission?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1165",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce4305b5a3de9a04ccab4447cb40611655490206c595db76b577f5e652bba1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce4305b5a3de-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Are there any specific arguments with respect to 906? I think that question was related to 906 and the certification there."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1165",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce4305b5a3de9a04ccab4447cb40611655490206c595db76b577f5e652bba1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce4305b5a3de-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: Well, where do you get that from the text? I mean, I understand how you get your principal argument from the text. But, there, I understood you to be saying that there are limits on the ways in which an omission can make statements in the MD&A or in the broader form misleading, and I don't see anything like that in the text."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Coberly",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1165",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce4305b5a3de9a04ccab4447cb40611655490206c595db76b577f5e652bba1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce4305b5a3de-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. COBERLY: -- including potentially the application of the safe harbor, because I actually think that statement that Your Honor is positing probably is a forward-looking statement that would be protected from liability under the statutory safe harbor."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "David C. Frederick",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1165",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce4305b5a3de9a04ccab4447cb40611655490206c595db76b577f5e652bba1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce4305b5a3de-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. FREDERICK: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: This case involves a classic 10b-5(b) misleading half-truth. Petitioners disclosed a few known trends that would affect their bottom line but omitted the IMO 2020 uncertainty that would decimate 40 percent of their revenue."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1165",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce4305b5a3de9a04ccab4447cb40611655490206c595db76b577f5e652bba1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce4305b5a3de-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 29,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: But, even if you lose, doesn't the SEC have authority over the omission?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1165",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce4305b5a3de9a04ccab4447cb40611655490206c595db76b577f5e652bba1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce4305b5a3de-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Mr. Frederick, what about the question that we agreed to review? Now you told us it was a worthless question in your brief in opposition. But, wisely or not, we took the case to decide that question."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1165",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce4305b5a3de9a04ccab4447cb40611655490206c595db76b577f5e652bba1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce4305b5a3de-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, the question is whether a failure to make a disclosure required under Item 303 can support a private claim under Section 10(b) -- you'll understand that to refer to 10b-5(b) -- \"even in the absence of an otherwise misleading statement.\""
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Frederick (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1165",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce4305b5a3de9a04ccab4447cb40611655490206c595db76b577f5e652bba1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce4305b5a3de-0002-0015",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We need that to get under 10b-5(b). We do that multiple ways, either categorically because the entire MD&A is false and misleading or because the individual statements within it are false and misleading."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1165",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce4305b5a3de9a04ccab4447cb40611655490206c595db76b577f5e652bba1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce4305b5a3de-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Frederick, am -- am I correct in assuming that the -- you're just adding, you're saying it has to be a pure"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Frederick (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1165",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce4305b5a3de9a04ccab4447cb40611655490206c595db76b577f5e652bba1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce4305b5a3de-0002-0016",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But the opacity of that last phrase that you highlighted, Justice Alito, is part of my argument. What is an otherwise misleading statement depends on context. The context here are the omissions."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1165",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce4305b5a3de9a04ccab4447cb40611655490206c595db76b577f5e652bba1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce4305b5a3de-0002-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF EPHRAIM McDOWELL FOR THE UNITED STATES, AS AMICUS CURIAE, SUPPORTING THE RESPONDENT"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "McDOWELL (United States)",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1165",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce4305b5a3de9a04ccab4447cb40611655490206c595db76b577f5e652bba1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce4305b5a3de-0002-0004",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I want to just pick up on this idea that the Petitioners are asking for a tight factual connection between the statement and -- and the omission. But that ignores the context in which an MD&A is -- is made. The context is Item 303."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1165",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce4305b5a3de9a04ccab4447cb40611655490206c595db76b577f5e652bba1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce4305b5a3de-0002-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 51,
        "start": 51
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "what's the difference between what you're saying and what Petitioner is saying."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Jackson",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1165",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce4305b5a3de9a04ccab4447cb40611655490206c595db76b577f5e652bba1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce4305b5a3de-0002-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I can see that. And I -- I think Ms. Coberly agreed, but I guess I'm trying to figure out the difference between the language of 10b-5 with respect to this issue and Section 11."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "McDOWELL (United States)",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1165",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce4305b5a3de9a04ccab4447cb40611655490206c595db76b577f5e652bba1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce4305b5a3de-0002-0008",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Second Circuit's decision is unpublished. It has fairly limited reasoning. And I read it to basically cite and adopt the Second Circuit's precedential opinion on this issue, which is Stratte-McClure."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "McDOWELL (United States)",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1165",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce4305b5a3de9a04ccab4447cb40611655490206c595db76b577f5e652bba1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce4305b5a3de-0002-0009",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The other -- the other point I wanted to make about the PSLRA's pleading standard is I think they're -- the other side is suggesting that that pleading standard can substantively limit the scope of subsection (b) of Rule 10b-5."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1165",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce4305b5a3de9a04ccab4447cb40611655490206c595db76b577f5e652bba1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce4305b5a3de-0002-0011",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 63,
        "start": 63
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, let me ask you the same question I asked -- I asked Mr. Frederick. What's your answer to the question on which we granted review? You changed the question. What's the question -- what's the answer to the question we agreed to review?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "McDOWELL (United States)",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1165",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce4305b5a3de9a04ccab4447cb40611655490206c595db76b577f5e652bba1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce4305b5a3de-0002-0012",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 64,
        "start": 64
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The answer is that an Item 303 violation can form the basis for a Rule 10b-5 claim."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1165",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce4305b5a3de9a04ccab4447cb40611655490206c595db76b577f5e652bba1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce4305b5a3de-0002-0013",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 66,
        "start": 66
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Commission enforces Item 303, correct?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "McDOWELL (United States)",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1165",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "ce4305b5a3de9a04ccab4447cb40611655490206c595db76b577f5e652bba1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce4305b5a3de-0002-0014",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 66,
        "start": 66
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "uncertain",
      "text": "The SEC has a few hundred employees that are tasked with reviewing tens of thousands of forms from registered companies each year, and it's simply not realistic to think that those employees will be able to routinely detect, investigate, and penalize the many disclosure violations that are taking place."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1165",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce4305b5a3de9a04ccab4447cb40611655490206c595db76b577f5e652bba1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce4305b5a3de-0003-0004",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 67,
        "start": 67
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the difference here is between enforcing it pursuant to Section 13 as opposed to Rule 10b-5, and there are greater penalties that the SEC can seek when they go under Rule 10b-5."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1165",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce4305b5a3de9a04ccab4447cb40611655490206c595db76b577f5e652bba1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce4305b5a3de-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 68,
        "start": 67
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Your Honor, I thought it was revealing that counsel is persisting in the argument that any failure to comply with Item 303 is actionable because it makes the entire MD&A misleading. Every company has to file"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Moab",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1165",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce4305b5a3de9a04ccab4447cb40611655490206c595db76b577f5e652bba1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce4305b5a3de-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 70,
        "start": 69
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Now counsel for Moab argue that they did identify specific statements, and that's very interesting because the brief in opposition doesn't mention them. Neither did the paragraphs in the complaint that purported to state this theory based on Item 303."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1165",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce4305b5a3de9a04ccab4447cb40611655490206c595db76b577f5e652bba1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce4305b5a3de-0003-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 72,
        "start": 72
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: The case is submitted."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1165",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce4305b5a3de9a04ccab4447cb40611655490206c595db76b577f5e652bba1dd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce4305b5a3de-0003-0003",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 72,
        "start": 72
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And we think the Court needs to vacate."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1165",
